---------Usage in Powershell Adminstrator mode:-----------

# Example: default locations
powershell -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -File C:\cs2-ds\set-system-tase-env.ps1 -AddCssharpDirToPath

# Example: custom client path \/

------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
powershell -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -File C:\cs2-ds\set-system-tase-env.ps1 `
  -ServerRoot "C:\cs2-ds" `
  -ClientRoot "E:\SteamLibrary\steamapps\common\Counter-Strike Global Offensive" `
  -AddCssharpDirToPath
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Verify (from any new PowerShell) \/

[Environment]::GetEnvironmentVariable('CS2_SERVER_ROOT','Machine')
[Environment]::GetEnvironmentVariable('CSSHARP_DIR','Machine')
[Environment]::GetEnvironmentVariable('CS2_CLIENT_ROOT','Machine')
[Environment]::GetEnvironmentVariable('TASE_PANO_ROOT','Machine')

Note: Existing shells won’t auto-refresh their copy; open a new PowerShell, or in the current one you can “pull” machine values into the process with:

$env:CS2_SERVER_ROOT = [Environment]::GetEnvironmentVariable('CS2_SERVER_ROOT','Machine')
$env:CSSHARP_DIR     = [Environment]::GetEnvironmentVariable('CSSHARP_DIR','Machine')
$env:CS2_CLIENT_ROOT = [Environment]::GetEnvironmentVariable('CS2_CLIENT_ROOT','Machine')
$env:TASE_PANO_ROOT  = [Environment]::GetEnvironmentVariable('TASE_PANO_ROOT','Machine')

Optional: Unset script (cleanup)
# C:\cs2-ds\unset-system-tase-env.ps1 (run as Admin)
[Environment]::SetEnvironmentVariable('CS2_SERVER_ROOT',$null,'Machine')
[Environment]::SetEnvironmentVariable('CSSHARP_DIR',$null,'Machine')
[Environment]::SetEnvironmentVariable('CS2_CLIENT_ROOT',$null,'Machine')
[Environment]::SetEnvironmentVariable('TASE_PANO_ROOT',$null,'Machine')
# (Optionally remove from PATH—manual/with care to avoid truncating)

With these machine-level vars in place, your compile.ps1 (and any other scripts) can just read CSSHARP_DIR, CS2_SERVER_ROOT, CS2_CLIENT_ROOT, and TASE_PANO_ROOT without worrying about per-window scope—exactly like traditional C++ SDK environment setups.